+Below is an explanation of the BFS approach to determine the fewest number of perfect squares that sum to a given positive integer n, followed by a complete JavaScript solution that can run directly in a JavaScript Runner environment.
+
+Explanation of BFS approach:
+1. We use a queue to perform a level-based search from 0 to n.
+2. Each element in the queue consists of the current sum and the number of steps (perfect squares) taken to reach that sum.
+3. We enqueue the starting position: (0, 0).
+4. While the queue is not empty:
+   • Dequeue an element (currentSum, steps).
+   • Generate new sums by adding each square number k² to currentSum where k² ≤ n.
+   • If the new sum equals n, we return steps+1 immediately (BFS ensures this is the minimum count).
+   • Otherwise, if the new sum is not visited, enqueue (newSum, steps+1).
+5. Once we reach n, we know the number of perfect squares used is minimal.
+
+```javascript
+// No external libraries are needed, so this code runs directly in a JavaScript Runner.
+
+function fewestSquaresBFS(n) {
+  if (n <= 0) return 0;
+
+  // Queue will hold [sum, steps]
+  const queue = [[0, 0]];
+  const visited = new Array(n+1).fill(false);
+  visited[0] = true;
+
+  while (queue.length > 0) {
+    const [currentSum, steps] = queue.shift();
+
+    // Try all potential squares k^2 up to n
+    for (let k = 1; k * k <= n; k++) {
+      const newSum = currentSum + k * k;
+      if (newSum === n) {
+        return steps + 1;  // BFS ensures this is the first time we reach n with the fewest squares
+      }
+      if (newSum < n && !visited[newSum]) {
+        visited[newSum] = true;
+        queue.push([newSum, steps + 1]);
+      }
+    }
+  }
+  return -1; // This shouldn't happen if n > 0
+}
+
+// Test the function with various inputs:
+const testValues = [12, 13, 25, 1, 2, 3, 10, 99];
+testValues.forEach(val => {
+  console.log(`n = ${val}, fewest squares = ${fewestSquaresBFS(val)}`);
+});
+```
