+### Removing Duplicate Letters from a String
+#### Problem Statement
+Given a string, remove duplicate letters to produce the smallest lexicographical result with unique characters.
+
+#### Approach
+We will use a stack-based approach to solve this problem. The idea is to iterate over the string and push characters onto the stack if they are not already present. If a character is smaller than the top of the stack and it appears later in the string, we pop the top of the stack and push the current character.
+
+#### Analysis
+The problem requires us to remove duplicate letters from a string to produce the smallest lexicographical result with unique characters. We can achieve this by using a stack-based approach. We will iterate over the string and push characters onto the stack if they are not already present. If a character is smaller than the top of the stack and it appears later in the string, we pop the top of the stack and push the current character.
+
+#### Design Decisions
+We will use a `std::string` to represent the input string and the result string. We will also use a `std::unordered_set` to keep track of the characters that are already present in the result string. We will iterate over the input string using a range-based loop and use the `std::string::push_back` method to add characters to the result string.
+
+#### Code
+```cpp
+#include <iostream>
+#include <string>
+#include <unordered_set>
+
+// Function to remove duplicate letters from a string
+std::string removeDuplicateLetters(const std::string& s) {
+    int count[26] = {0};
+    bool visited[26] = {false};
+    std::string result = "";
+
+    // Count the frequency of each character
+    for (char c : s) {
+        count[c - 'a']++;
+    }
+
+    for (char c : s) {
+        count[c - 'a']--;
+        if (visited[c - 'a']) {
+            continue;
+        }
+
+        // Remove characters from the result that are larger than the current character
+        // and appear later in the string
+        while (!result.empty() && result.back() > c && count[result.back() - 'a'] > 0) {
+            visited[result.back() - 'a'] = false;
+            result.pop_back();
+        }
+
+        result += c;
+        visited[c - 'a'] = true;
+    }
+
+    return result;
+}
+
+int main() {
+    // Test cases
+    std::string testCases[] = {"bcabc", "cbacdcbc", "abc"};
+    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
+        std::cout << "Test Case " << i + 1 << ": Input = " << testCases[i] << std::endl;
+        std::cout << "Output: " << removeDuplicateLetters(testCases[i]) << std::endl;
+        std::cout << std::endl;
+    }
+
+    return 0;
+}
+```
+
+#### Explanation
