+Below is the detailed explanation and the complete C++ program:
+
+Explanation:
+-------------
+The program implements a solution for finding the k closest elements to a target in a sorted array using binary search followed by a two-pointer approach. The logic is as follows:
+
+1. Use std::lower_bound to get the insertion position for the target value in the sorted array. This provides an O(log n) search time.
+2. Initialize two pointers: one to the left of the insertion position (left pointer) and one at the insertion position (right pointer).
+3. Iteratively compare the elements on both sides of the target:
+   - If one side is out of bounds, take the element from the other side.
+   - Otherwise compare the absolute difference with the target. On ties, choose the left element.
+4. Collect exactly k elements.
+5. Finally, sort the selected k elements (because the two-pointer approach might not produce a sorted list) and output them in ascending order as space-separated values.
+
+The code below is self-contained in one C++ file, uses modern C++ features, provides multiple test cases within the main function, and prints the results to stdout.
+
+Below is the complete C++ program:
+
+```cpp
+#include <iostream>
+#include <vector>
+#include <algorithm>
+#include <iterator>
+
+// Function to find the k closest elements to a given target in a sorted array.
+std::vector<int> findKClosestElements(const std::vector<int>& arr, int k, int target) {
+    int n = static_cast<int>(arr.size());
+    
+    // Find the insertion position using std::lower_bound (binary search)
+    int pos = static_cast<int>(std::lower_bound(arr.begin(), arr.end(), target) - arr.begin());
+    
+    // Two pointers: one to the left of pos and one at pos.
+    int left = pos - 1, right = pos;
+    
+    // Vector for storing the k closest elements.
+    std::vector<int> result;
+    
+    // Select k closest elements using two-pointer approach.
+    while (result.size() < static_cast<size_t>(k)) {
+        if (left < 0) {
+            result.push_back(arr[right++]);
+        } else if (right >= n) {
+            result.push_back(arr[left--]);
+        } else {
+            // Compare the absolute differences.
+            if (target - arr[left] <= arr[right] - target) {
+                result.push_back(arr[left--]);
+            } else {
+                result.push_back(arr[right++]);
+            }
+        }
+    }
+    
+    // Sorting the result to maintain ascending order.
+    std::sort(result.begin(), result.end());
+    return result;
+}
+
