+The approach you described—starting at the first element, tracking an increasing sequence, and restarting whenever a smaller number is encountered—only finds a local increasing sequence. This technique misses cases where a longer increasing subsequence might start at an element later in the sequence.
+
+For example, consider the array: [5, 6, 1, 2, 3]. A greedy restart when you see the number 1 would give you a local subsequence [5, 6] or [1, 2, 3], but the best answer here is [1, 2, 3] with length 3.
+
+To solve the problem correctly, one common solution is a dynamic programming approach using binary search (often called the "patience sorting" technique) that guarantees an O(n log n) time complexity. In this method, we maintain an auxiliary array (dp) to store the smallest tail element of an increasing subsequence for each possible length. For each number, we use binary search to find the position at which it can extend an existing subsequence or start a new one. In the end, the length of the dp array is the length of the longest strictly increasing subsequence over the entire sequence.
+
+Below is a complete, self-contained JavaScript code block that includes this approach with sample test cases. You can run it directly in a JavaScript environment (e.g., using Node.js or a browser console).
+
+```javascript
+// Function to compute the length of the longest strictly increasing subsequence using binary search
+function lengthOfLIS(nums) {
+  if (!nums || nums.length === 0) return 0;
+  
+  let dp = []; // dp[i] holds the smallest tail value for an increasing subsequence of length i+1
+  
+  for (const num of nums) {
+    let left = 0;
+    let right = dp.length;
+    
+    // Use binary search to find the right position for num in dp
+    while (left < right) {
+      const mid = Math.floor((left + right) / 2);
+      if (dp[mid] < num) {
+        left = mid + 1;
+      } else {
+        right = mid;
+      }
+    }
+    
+    // If left is equal to dp length, num is greater than any element in dp so far
+    if (left === dp.length) {
+      dp.push(num);
+    } else {
+      dp[left] = num; // replace to maintain the smallest possible ending value
+    }
+  }
+  
+  return dp.length;
+}
+
+// Test cases to validate the solution
+const testCases = [
+  {
+    input: [10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7, 101, 18],
+    expected: 4, // The longest increasing subsequence is [2, 3, 7, 101]
+  },
+  {
+    input: [0, 1, 0, 3, 2, 3],
+    expected: 4, // One longest increasing subsequence is [0, 1, 2, 3]
+  },
+  {
+    input: [7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7, 7],
+    expected: 1, // All numbers are the same so maximum length is 1
+  },
+  {
+    input: [5, 6, 1, 2, 3],
+    expected: 3, // The longest increasing subsequence is [1, 2, 3]
+  },
+];
+
+// Run each test case and log the results
+testCases.forEach((test, index) => {
+  const result = lengthOfLIS(test.input);
+  console.log(`Test case #${index + 1}:`);
+  console.log(`Input: [${test.input.join(', ')}]`);
+  console.log(`Expected Output: ${test.expected}`);
+  console.log(`Computed Output: ${result}`);
+  console.log('---------------------------------------------');
+});
+```
+
+Explanation of the Code:
+
+1. The function lengthOfLIS iterates through each number in the given array.
+2. It uses binary search in an auxiliary array (dp) to efficiently determine the location at which the current number belongs. The dp array is maintained in a way that dp[i] holds the smallest possible ending number for an increasing subsequence of length i+1.
+3. If the current number is greater than all elements in dp, it appends the number, thereby increasing the length of the known increasing subsequence. Otherwise, it replaces the smallest element that is greater than or equal to the current number.
+4. Finally, the length of dp gives the length of the longest strictly increasing subsequence.
+
+This solution correctly handles all cases and efficiently computes the desired length.
