+Explanation:
+In this solution, we add a helper function, printFormatted, which prints a long long integer with commas inserted as thousand separators. The function works recursively by dividing the number by 1000 until the value is less than 1000. Then it prints the parts with the appropriate formatting. Below is the complete, self-contained C program.
+
+```c
+#include <stdio.h>
+
+void printFormatted(long long n) {
+    if(n < 1000) {
+        printf("%lld", n);
+    } else {
+        // Recursively print the number before the last 3 digits.
+        printFormatted(n / 1000);
+        // Then print the last 3 digits with leading zeros if necessary.
+        printf(",%03lld", n % 1000);
+    }
+}
+
+int main() {
+    // Print column headers
+    printf("%-10s %-40s\n", "Number", "Factorial");
+
+    // Loop through numbers 1 to 20
+    for (int i = 1; i <= 20; i++) {
+        long long factorial = 1;
+        for (int j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
+            factorial *= j;
+        }
+        // Print number
+        printf("%-10d ", i);
+        // Print factorial with commas inserted
+        printFormatted(factorial);
+        printf("\n");
+    }
+    return 0;
+}
+```
+
+Explanation Details:
+1. The printFormatted function uses recursion to split the number at every thousand. When the number is less than 1000, it prints normally. Otherwise, it recursively prints the higher order digits, then prints the last three digits, ensuring they are padded with zeros if required.
+2. In main(), we calculate the factorial from 1 to 20 using a simple loop.
+3. The output is formatted into two columns: one for the number and one for the factorial with commas for better readability.
